#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/id #uuid "283001f5-f9e4-5990-a900-318677a48601" :question/math-academy-id "e-3740" :question/problem "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3 -3x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} - 3 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & > 0 \\\\ x & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "006fe48a-9c8e-568b-90f1-40b12dc338bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3741" :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2x^{3} - x^{4} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 6x^{2} - 4x^{3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x - 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 12x^{2} & > 0 \\\\ 12x^{2} - 12x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/b2ab07481181ff8e94caf76114a28445.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1$. So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,1)$.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) < 0$. By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1) > 0$, which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "99e07b9d-aaaa-54b6-9118-78df023261f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3745" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x)$, where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/bbbc1786039bf107ff38f347061b540d.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x)$, we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5)$. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863515ff-8ffa-5b28-9838-02b3f2ee569d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183005} {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} {:answer/id #uuid "e526471f-734d-50c7-8111-39708dfcf1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- ∞,-4)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183007} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b7fc8b-8aa3-5225-8b1a-b1142bd31a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answe